        "description": "<div id=\"remove_no_follow\">\n\t\t<div class=\"grid grid--cols-10@md grid--cols-8@lg article-column\">\n\t\t\t\t\t  <div class=\"col-12 col-10@md col-6@lg col-start-3@lg\">\n\t\t\t\t\t\t<div class=\"article-column__content\">\n<section class=\"wp-block-bigbite-multi-title\"><div class=\"container\"></div></section>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">A new Windows update does not automatically mean that every user receives the same features on the same day. Microsoft rolls out many new features in stages. Even if a feature is technically already part of the installed version of Windows, it may initially remain disabled on your PC.</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">There\u2019s a method to this: With phased rollouts, Microsoft can initially deliver new features to a subset of users and monitor whether any issues arise. If errors occur, the rollout can be halted or adjusted before millions of computers are affected. Some new features are also only enabled in specific versions of Windows or in certain regions.</p>\n\n\n\n<p",
